# Every kind of crash, as a program (roadmap os-server.md P12): the kernel
# stops it, the OS server ends it by its fault's signal and says so on its
# terminal, and the shell goes on (crash alone lists the kinds; #TS's
# SIGSEGV is the last status, 139). The 287's error is the kernel's fault
# #MF for a process, SIGFPE: on Bochs alone, as MAME's AT has no 287.
program: sh
# DOSBox-X lets a write through CS go through (docs/emulators.md), and has
# no drive C.
emulators: bochs mame
install: C /BIN/CRASH z-crash.rx
keys: crash\ncrash gp\ncrash ud\ncrash de\ncrash ss\ncrash io\ncrash cli\n
keys: crash hlt\ncrash int\ncrash kernel\ncrash call\ncrash write\ncrash fpu\n
keys: crash tf\ncrash nt\necho $?\n
timeout: 60
---
rx286, test build
/a$ crash
crash what? The kinds: gp ud de ss io cli hlt int kernel call write fpu tf nt
/a$ crash gp
crash: jump past the end of my code
rx286: crash[11] fault #GP(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_gp}
/a$ crash ud
crash: an instruction that does not exist
rx286: crash[12] fault #UD at 0007:{z:crash.crash_ud}
/a$ crash de
crash: divide by zero
rx286: crash[13] fault #DE at 0007:{z:crash.crash_de}
/a$ crash ss
crash: push past the end of my stack
rx286: crash[14] fault #SS(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_ss_push}
/a$ crash io
crash: read the keyboard's port myself
rx286: crash[15] fault #GP(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_io}
/a$ crash cli
crash: turn interrupts off
rx286: crash[16] fault #GP(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_cli}
/a$ crash hlt
crash: halt the CPU
rx286: crash[17] fault #GP(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_hlt}
/a$ crash int
crash: INT 32h, beyond the IDT
rx286: crash[18] fault #GP(0192) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_int}
/a$ crash kernel
crash: load the kernel's data selector
rx286: crash[19] fault #GP(0010) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_kernel}
/a$ crash call
crash: call into the kernel's code
rx286: crash[20] fault #GP(0008) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_call}
/a$ crash write
crash: write over my own code
rx286: crash[21] fault #GP(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_write}
/a$ crash fpu
crash: divide by zero on the 287, unmasked
@bochs rx286: crash[22] fault #MF at 0007:{z:crash.crash_fpu_after}
@mame crash: no 287 error arrived; is there a 287?
/a$ crash tf
crash: set the trap flag
rx286: crash[23] fault #DB at 0007:{z:crash.crash_tf_after}
/a$ crash nt
crash: set NT and IRET
rx286: crash[24] fault #TS(0000) at 0007:{z:crash.crash_nt_iret}
/a$ echo $?
139
/a$
